uld accumulate everything in bytes, then only convert to the display unit at the very end. But I'm realizing there's a subtlety with how GNU du handles rounding — it accumulates the raw block counts and converts the total at display time, not per-file. So I need to track whether I'm accumulating in 512-byte blocks or actual bytes, then apply the final conversion consistently. I'm simplifying this by always accumulating sizes in bytes—using a helper function to convert block counts to bytes for disk usage mode, then formatting the final total based on the requested unit at display time.\n\nImplementing the human-readable size formatter... I'm checking how GNU du actually formats human-readable sizes—it uses binary base 1024 with K, M, G, T, P, E, Z, Y suffixes, and applies different formatting rules depending on the magnitude (one decimal place for values under 10, integers for 10 and above). But the requirements specify using pkg/format.HumanSize, which consistently formats all suffixed values with one decimal place.\n\nSince I can't import that package, I need to replicate its behavior exactly, though I'm concerned the differential tests against gdu output might fail if my implementation diverges. Let me examine the gnulib human_readable function more closely to understand the exact formatting rules I need to match. I'm refocusing on the actual acceptance criteria rather than trying to perfectly match GNU du's behavior. The requirements are straightforward: the tool needs to compile, show subdirectory sizes in 1024-byte blocks, support the -s, -c, and -h flags, handle hard-link deduplication, and report permission errors. Let me implement the humanSize function to match the pkg/format pattern and get the core functionality working. I need to finalize the implementation and clarify the output format from the requirements—SIZE should be a right-aligned integer with no padding, just a tab separator before the path. I'm also thinking through how symlinks interact with recursion: we should traverse into real directories but skip symlink directories entirely, while still counting their disk usage in the parent.\n\nFor the platform-specific type differences, I'm noting that `Dev` has different sizes on Darwin versus Linux, but since I'm only comparing values within the same run with consistent conversions, this shouldn't cause issues.\n\nWriting the implementation... I'm comparing the struct field layouts across different platforms—Darwin uses different type sizes and field orderings than Linux, and even Linux varies between amd64 and arm64 architectures.\n\nThe key insight is that `Dev` changes types across platforms, so I'll normalize everything to `uint64` for consistency. The other fields like `Blocks`, `Size`, and `Ino` are already consistent, which simplifies things. I should also account for the case where the input is a file rather than a directory, similar to how GNU du handles it.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the error handling—Go's `os` package already formats errors with the path and system message, so I can just prefix them with \"du: \" to match GNU du's style closely enough. I'm looking at how os.Lstat errors format and comparing them to GNU du's output—the Go error includes the path automatically, so I need to decide whether to extract it separately or just use the error message as-is. The acceptance criteria doesn't specify exact formatting, so I'll go with a simpler approach that formats the error message consistently.\n\nWriting the du implementation... Setting up the main function with a SIGPIPE handler and then defining all the command-line flags that correspond to the du utility's options like all files, summarize, total, max depth, and various size display modes. Setting up command-line flags...\n\nNow I'm resolving the size display mode based on which flags were provided, defaulting to kilobytes but switching to megabytes, human-readable, or byte mode depending on what the user specified. I'm also handling the