t. I see the issue—using `filepath.Join` normalizes paths in a way that creates inconsistency with GNU du's output. When the argument is `.`, the top-level shows as `.` but children become `subdir` instead of `./subdir`, which breaks test compatibility. I need to implement a custom `joinPath` function that preserves the parent directory's prefix form to maintain consistency.\n\nWriting the joinPath helper...\n\nNow I'm testing this against different path formats to make sure it handles the `.` prefix correctly while also working for regular directories and those with trailing slashes. The key insight is that when the root argument is `.`, child paths should preserve that `./` prefix, but I also need to verify how the root path itself gets printed in the output. Writing the final implementation...\n\nI'm realizing there's a potential inefficiency with double-stating when `os.ReadDir` already handles DT_UNKNOWN cases, but for the common case where d_type is available, my `os.Lstat` call is the only stat needed. I could optimize by using `entry.Info()` to get cached FileInfo, but I'd still need the underlying `Stat_t` data, and the behavior of `entry.Info()` across different scenarios makes this tricky.\n\nLooking at the Go documentation, `DirEntry.Info()` returns the FileInfo from the most recent stat call and uses lstat for symlinks, so it's reliable—but since it may or may not actually call lstat depending on caching, sticking with an explicit `os.Lstat` is cleaner and more predictable.\n\nMy error handling already accounts for partial entries when `os.ReadDir` fails, and since I'm not following symlinks, circular symlinks won't be an issue.\n\nHard links to directories aren't a concern since filesystems don't allow them except for `.` and `..`, which `os.ReadDir` filters out anyway. Mount points work fine too—different devices have different (Dev, Ino) pairs, so deduplication stays correct, and I traverse into mounted directories like GNU du does by default.\n\nNow