hould be `uint64` across different platforms like Darwin and Linux.\n\nNow I'm checking the `Dev` field behavior across systems—on Darwin it's `int32` while Linux uses `uint64`, so I'll need to cast it appropriately for the map key.\n\nI also need to revisit the `-s` and `-a` flag interaction since they should be mutually exclusive according to the requirements, and I want to make sure I'm handling that correctly.\n\nWriting the complete implementation... I'm weighing whether to use `filepath.WalkDir` for its efficiency benefits or stick with manual recursion to keep the bottom-up printing logic straightforward. The key trade-off is that `filepath.WalkDir` avoids unnecessary stat calls on directory entries, but it visits directories top-down, which would require buffering the entire tree in memory to print in the right order—manual recursion handles this more naturally.\n\nLooking at my implementation, I'm using `os.ReadDir` which also returns cached `DirEntry` objects, and I only call `os.Lstat` on files and symlinks, not directories themselves. This means the performance is essentially equivalent to what `filepath.WalkDir` would give me, so the manual approach is justified.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the code and just need to decide between `os.FileInfo` and `fs.FileInfo` for the type system.\n\nChecking my imports, I realize I replaced `filepath.Join` with my own `joinPath` function, so I might not actually need the `path/filepath` import at all.\n\nLooking at how GNU du handles paths, it preserves the exact argument format for the root entry and concatenates it with child names. I'm implementing a simple string concatenation approach...\n\nWriting path joining logic... Writing the Go package... Defining the size mode constants and setting up the main function with signal handling...\n\nNow I'm declaring all the command-line flags for the du utility, mapping both short and long forms to their respective boolean and integer variables, following the POSIX specification for disk usage reporting options. Setting up the display mode flags...\n\nNow I'm parsing the command-line arguments and resolving which display format to use, with byte mode taking priority over human-readable, which takes priority over megabytes, which takes priority over kilobytes.\n\nHandling the summarize flag and depth settings...\n\nIf the summarize flag is set, I'm overriding the max depth to zero to show only the total. When no arguments are provided, I default to the current directory, then I'm iterating through each argument to process it while maintaining a deduplication map across all of them and accumulating the grand total.\n\nNow I need to output the grand total if requested, then exit with the appropriate code. After that, I'm defining the processArg function which takes a file path and processes it, starting by getting the file stats and handling any errors that occur.\n\nFor non-directory arguments, I'm calculating the size, checking if we've already seen this file (to avoid counting hard links twice), and printing it out before returning. If it's a directory, I delegate to the walkDir function to recursively process its contents.\n\nNow I'm implementing the recursive directory traversal function, which starts by getting the directory's own metadata and checking if we've already processed it to handle hard links correctly.\n\nReading the directory contents and iterating through each entry, recursively processing subdirectories while collecting file sizes, and handling any read errors along the way. Finishing the directory traversal logic... Continuing with the depth checking logic, then moving into path construction and size formatting utilities. Writing the humanSize function...\n\nNow I'm checking the implementation against all the acceptance criteria—the directory walking logic handles per-subdirectory output, the maxDepth flag controls suppression, the total flag adds the grand total line, human-readable sizes are formatted with the approp